/-!
# Local regular equations for a closed immersion of smooth schemes

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tags 067U and 063J, and More on Morphisms, Tag 0FUE,
first proof paragraph (blueprint: lem-pur-smooth-pair-regular-immersion).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A closed immersion between schemes smooth over the same arbitrary base has affine-local
regular equations at each point of its source. The quotient-spectrum isomorphism commutes
with the maps to the ambient affine neighbourhood, so it identifies closed subschemes.
The list length is the nonnegative integer `b`, including the empty list.
Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tags 067U and 063J; More on Morphisms, Tag 0FUE,
first proof paragraph (blueprint: lem-pur-smooth-pair-regular-immersion).

Proof:
1. Around the image of `y`, choose affine neighbourhoods in the base and the ambient scheme.
The closed immersion gives a quotient `C = A/I` (def-weil-closed-immersion). The two restricted
structure maps remain smooth (def-sbc-smooth-morphisms), hence the corresponding ring maps
are smooth (lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-iff-smooth-affine). Thus both algebras are finitely
presented and flat (def-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map, lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-flat).
2. The quotient is finitely presented as an `A`-algebra by
lem-weil-pbc-finite-presentation-over-finite-type-algebra. Applying
lem-weil-pbc-finite-presentation-surjection-kernel-fg with zero polynomial variables shows
that `I` is finitely generated; no Noetherian hypothesis on the base is needed.
3. On the fibre through `y`, the ambient and quotient local rings are regular by
lem-weil-pbc-smooth-fibre-regular-local. By
lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-quotient-kernel-parameters the fibre ideal is generated by a prefix
of a regular system of parameters. This prefix is regular by
lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-generators-regular-sequence: every successive injection is one
from the full sequence, and its final quotient surjects onto the full nonzero quotient.
4. Lift these generators to elements `f` of `I`, clearing denominators outside the prime.
The resulting unit multiples preserve regularity and the prefix ideals. Flatness of `C`
and lem-weil-pbc-flat-quotient-tensor-injective identify the fibre ideal with the tensor
of `I`. Localize this identification (lem-weil-pbc-localization-module-exact).
Nakayama (lem-weil-pbc-nakayama-local-generators, empty family) shows that the lifts generate
`I` at the point. Invert a product of finitely many denominators to obtain equality of
ideals on an affine neighbourhood. Principal localization preserves flatness and finite
presentation; a presentation adjoins a variable and the relation `hu - 1`.
5. The fibre algebra is Noetherian (lem-wf-affine-noetherian-components). Spread the regular
sequence from its local ring to a principal neighbourhood using
lem-exc-regular-sequence-principal-neighbourhood. A fibre element is a fraction `a/r`,
with `r` from the base outside its prime, so this shrink lifts to the ambient principal
open defined by `a`. Equality of ideals persists.
6. Lift regularity from the fibre inductively on the prefix length. The zero-prefix
quotient is flat and locally finitely presented over the chosen base affine. If the
prefix quotient has these properties, its next principal equation cuts out a Cartier
divisor on the fibre near `y`. Apply lem-pc-recd-fibre-cartier-local. Shrink the ambient
affine so its intersection with the prefix quotient lies in the resulting open. By
def-pc-relative-effective-cartier and def-nagata-effective-cartier-ideal the next quotient
is flat, and its ideal has local nonzerodivisor generators. The prescribed equation itself
is a nonzerodivisor: if `(f) = (v)` and `v` is a nonzerodivisor, write `f = uv`, `v = wf`;
cancelling `v` gives `wu = 1`. Stalkwise injectivity implies injectivity on sections.
Previous injections survive localization (lem-weil-pbc-localization-module-exact).
Finite presentation persists by adjoining the finitely many prefix equations and the
localization relation (def-sbc-locally-finite-presentation); flatness is interpreted
stalkwise by def-weil-flat-morphism. This completes the induction.
7. The ideal equality identifies the base change (def-pc-base-change) with the quotient
spectrum over the affine neighbourhood. Every successive multiplication is injective.
The final quotient is nonzero since it has the retained point `y`, giving
RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular (def-weil-pbc-regular-sequence). When the list is empty,
step 4 makes the ideal zero near the point and the induction has no steps. -/
theorem exists_regular_equations_of_smooth_closedImmersion {S X Y : Scheme.{u}}
    (p : X ⟶ S) (q : Y ⟶ S) (i : Y ⟶ X) [Smooth p] [Smooth q]
    [IsClosedImmersion i] (hi : i ≫ p = q) (y : Y) :
    ∃ U : X.Opens, i y ∈ U ∧
      ∃ (A : CommRingCat.{u}) (a : U.toScheme ≅ Spec A) (f : List A),
        RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular A f ∧
        ∃ e : pullback i U.ι ≅ Spec (CommRingCat.of (A ⧸ Ideal.ofList f)),
          e.hom ≫ Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (Ideal.Quotient.mk (Ideal.ofList f))) =
            pullback.snd i U.ι ≫ a.hom := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
